# Canvas
@summary Imperative 2D drawing — circles, rectangles, lines, text, animation
@category Canvas

The Canvas module provides immediate-mode 2D drawing. You open a window with canvas(width, height), issue draw calls, and call show() to keep the window alive until closed. Canvas functions are impure: they mutate window state and the framebuffer.

For pure scene description (compose draw commands without touching the framebuffer), see the spn.canvas.draw module — it gives you a typed DrawCmd algebra that render() interprets at the action boundary.

## Setup and lifecycle

canvas(width, height) — open the canvas window. Call once at the top of your script.

show() — block on the canvas window until the user closes it. Without show() the window flashes briefly and disappears when the script returns.

animate(fps, drawFn) — run the named function once per frame at the given rate. drawFn receives the elapsed seconds as a float; the loop returns when the user closes the window.

## Drawing primitives

clear(r, g, b) — fill the entire canvas with a color. RGB components are floats in 0.0..1.0.

fill(r, g, b), stroke(r, g, b) — set fill and stroke colors for subsequent shapes.

strokeWeight(w) — set the stroke width in pixels.

rect(x, y, w, h) — axis-aligned rectangle at (x, y).

circle(x, y, r) — circle centered at (x, y) with radius r.

line(x1, y1, x2, y2) — line segment.

text(x, y, content, size) — render text. Size is a font scale factor.

## Coordinate system

The origin (0, 0) is the top-left corner. X increases right, Y increases down. Sizes are in pixels.

## Writable images

For pixel-level work — function plots, fractals, image filters, NN visualizations, anything you'd want to also save as a file — Canvas provides a writable image type that is independent of the canvas window.

image(width, height) — allocate a new writable image. Returns an Image value.

setPixel(image, x, y, r, g, b) — write one pixel. RGB is in 0.0..1.0; out-of-range values clamp; out-of-bounds coords are ignored. Cheap for sparse writes; expensive at millions of calls.

mapPixels(image, fn) — fill every pixel by calling fn(x, y) which must return a 3-tuple (r, g, b). The Java loop avoids per-pixel SPN dispatch overhead — only the callback crosses the boundary.

There are two natural shapes for the callback:

- A pure lambda (x, y) -> (r, g, b) — each pixel computed independently, no shared state. Fits gradients, function plots, fractals, anything where pixel N doesn't depend on pixel < N.

- A do() closure inside a stateful block — the closure captures the enclosing this and can read or mutate its fields between pixel calls. Iteration is row-major and sequential, so accumulators, running averages, and IIR-style filters work as expected.

drawImage(image, x, y) — queue the image for display in the canvas window at the given top-left position. Multiple drawImage calls of the same Image share a cached GL texture; mutations between draws trigger a re-upload automatically.

savePng(image, path) — write the image to a PNG file. Works without ever calling canvas() — useful for headless rendering.

## Pure mode

If you prefer not to mix computation with drawing effects, build an Array of DrawCmd values using spn.canvas.draw and pass it to render() — that is the only function in draw.spn that touches the canvas. Pure scene functions are then memoizable, parallelizable, and trivially testable.

--- example: hello-canvas.spn
-- Minimal Canvas program: open a window, draw one shape, wait for close.
-- Top-level statements run as a script; no main() wrapper needed.
import Canvas

canvas(400, 300)
clear(0.10, 0.10, 0.15)

fill(0.85, 0.55, 0.20)
circle(200.0, 150.0, 60.0)

show()
---

--- example: shapes.spn
-- Mix of primitives with stroke + fill.
import Canvas

canvas(640, 360)
clear(0.08, 0.10, 0.14)

-- A filled rectangle with a contrasting outline.
stroke(0.95, 0.85, 0.40)
strokeWeight(3.0)
fill(0.20, 0.40, 0.70)
rect(40.0, 40.0, 200.0, 120.0)

-- A triangle from three line segments.
stroke(0.85, 0.30, 0.55)
strokeWeight(2.0)
line(320.0,  60.0, 460.0, 280.0)
line(460.0, 280.0, 540.0,  60.0)
line(540.0,  60.0, 320.0,  60.0)

-- A label.
fill(0.85, 0.85, 0.90)
text(40.0, 220.0, "shapes", 0.32)

show()
---

--- example: animation.spn
-- animate() drives a frame loop. The callback is a named action that
-- receives the elapsed seconds; close the window to stop.
import Canvas
import Math (cos, sin)

action drawFrame(float) -> int = (elapsed) {
    clear(0.06, 0.07, 0.10)

    let cx = 240.0 + cos(elapsed) * 120.0
    let cy = 160.0 + sin(elapsed * 1.7) * 80.0

    fill(0.95, 0.65, 0.25)
    circle(cx, cy, 24.0)

    fill(0.45, 0.65, 0.95)
    circle(480.0 - cx, 320.0 - cy, 18.0)

    0
}

canvas(480, 320)
animate(60.0, drawFrame)
show()
---

--- example: stateful-pixels.spn
-- Stateful mapPixels: the do() closure captures `this` from the
-- enclosing stateful block, letting iteration N depend on iteration < N.
--
-- Painter holds a running brightness value. Each pixel updates it via
-- a one-pole low-pass filter (prev = prev * 0.9 + target * 0.1),
-- producing horizontally-smoothed bands you couldn't get from a pure
-- lambda. Same image is drawn to the canvas and saved to disk.
import Canvas
import Math (sin, toFloat)

stateful type Painter(prev: float)

canvas(256, 256)

Painter(0.0) {
    let img = image(256, 256)

    mapPixels(img, do(x, y) {
        let target = sin(toFloat(x) * 0.05) * 0.5 + 0.5
        this.prev = this.prev * 0.9 + target * 0.1
        (this.prev, this.prev * 0.5, 1.0 - this.prev)
    })

    drawImage(img, 0.0, 0.0)
    savePng(img, "stateful-pixels.png")
}

show()
---

--- example: pure-scene.spn
-- Pure scene description with the spn.canvas.draw command algebra.
-- Functions return Array<DrawCmd>; render() interprets them.
import Array (concat)
import Canvas
import spn.canvas.draw

pure background() -> Array = () {
    [ CmdClear(0.10, 0.10, 0.13) ]
}

pure dot(float, float, float, float, float) -> Array = (x, y, r, g, b) {
    [ CmdFill(r, g, b), CmdCircle(x, y, 12.0) ]
}

pure scene() -> Array = () {
    let cmds = background()
    cmds = concat(cmds, dot(120.0, 100.0, 0.95, 0.55, 0.25))
    cmds = concat(cmds, dot(200.0, 100.0, 0.55, 0.85, 0.45))
    cmds = concat(cmds, dot(280.0, 100.0, 0.45, 0.65, 0.95))
    cmds
}

canvas(400, 200)
render(scene())
show()
---
